<p>European Union leaders meeting in Brussels on Thursday failed to persuade Hungarian Prime Minister Viktor Orbán to drop his veto of a planned €90 billion loan for Ukraine. That left the bloc without unanimity on a package that 25 EU countries want to start disbursing by early April. The dispute comes at a sensitive moment for Europe as it tries to sustain support for Kyiv while Hungary heads into an April 12 election, where Orbán has turned his standoff with Ukraine and the EU into a campaign issue at home. Orbán says Hungary will not back the loan until oil flows through the Druzhba pipeline resume after supplies via Ukraine were halted, a position Slovakia’s Prime Minister Robert Fico has also backed. Even with the deadlock, EU officials have signaled that they are exploring other ways forward. Foreign policy chief Kaja Kallas said the bloc has tools to bypass the veto if leaders decide to use them, underscoring that support for Ukraine remains a central goal for most member states.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/FAmexaKe9jh4AgAoAqaKbrvkWzlIathqoDraPBlBnRTdJBaxJejLKpgURLzGG9igRycZ2lSq2uv9muZmezcbOczVZwUUulzlcAiUSQCjflYQC98pHGh835IrXCDyPBsMvGfUHLJsu3cWG709kOQtpukUKbz4A9XYUuvpQqhNR7zj2djO1iuZcaLzeYQ-Z0cE7cU0e6zq86g' alt='Prime Minister Viktor Orban of Hungary in Budapest on Sunday. His disputes with President Volodymyr Zelensky of Ukraine have become a focal point of Hungary’s coming election.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Summit tensions: European Council President António Costa told Orbán his behavior was “unacceptable” and broke a core norm of EU cooperation, according to diplomats briefed on the closed-door meeting.</li><li>Escalation threat: Officials have so far avoided a “major confrontation” with Budapest, but some say a tougher response — including possible Article 7 discussions — could follow after Hungary’s April 12 election if Orbán stays in power.</li><li>Election backdrop: Diplomats told Politico that frustration with Orbán is rising, but many leaders do not want to get drawn into Hungarian domestic politics before the April 12 vote.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Viktor Orbán: Orbán casts the veto as a legitimate use of Hungary’s rights under EU rules and argues that Budapest should not approve the loan while its own oil-supply concerns remain unresolved. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/eu-no-deal-90-billion-ukraine-loan-viktor-orban-european-council/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>Kaja Kallas: Kallas says the standoff is now a test of political will inside the EU, arguing that the question is less whether tools exist than whether leaders are ready to use them. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/eu-has-tools-to-bypass-hungarys-ukraine-loan-veto-but-must-show-courage-kallas-says'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>António Costa: According to diplomats, the European Council president told Orbán that blocking the loan crossed a red line in EU cooperation and was unacceptable. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/eu-no-deal-90-billion-ukraine-loan-viktor-orban-european-council/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>Robert Fico: Fico has sided with Budapest, pointing to Slovakia’s loss of cheaper Russian fuel and what he sees as too little progress on restoring pipeline flows through Ukraine. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/eu-no-deal-90-billion-ukraine-loan-viktor-orban-european-council/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/19/world/europe/ukraine-hungary-orban-elections.html'>A Crucial Loan for Ukraine Becomes Election Leverage for Orban</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/eu-leaders-blast-viktor-orbn-ukraine-loan-veto-131210459'>EU leaders slam Orbán over Ukraine loan veto, accuse election games</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/news/635533-eu-hungary-ukraine-loan/'>EU threatens Hungary over Ukraine loan – Politico</a> - rt.com</li><li><a 